WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.135
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:04.403
is a two-time
Academy Award-winning actor

00:00:04.403 --> 00:00:06.439
you know from films
such as "Forrest Gump,"

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.140
"Philadelphia,"
"Saving Private Ryan,"

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.476
"Castaway," "Apollo 13,"
and so much more.

00:00:10.476 --> 00:00:12.545
He stars in the movie "Elvis,"

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:15.081
which is coming to theaters
June 24th.

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:16.416
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:18.117
-Is there a lawyer
representing the family?

00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:21.321
-Oh, unnecessary.
I am of the firm opinion

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:24.924
that family is the most
important thing in the world.

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:27.560
In mind of that,
I have taken the liberty

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.262
of making these contracts out

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:32.532
in the name of
Elvis Presley Enterprises --

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:33.866
a family business.

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:36.636
And I was thinking
Vernon Presley,

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:38.238
business manager.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.409
-What do you think, Daddy?

00:00:43.409 --> 00:00:45.878
-Oh, well, uh...

00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:47.212
I like it very much, son.

00:00:47.212 --> 00:00:49.114
-Ah!
[ Laughs ]

00:00:49.114 --> 00:00:50.883
-Please welcome to the show,
the one, the only,

00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:52.685
Tom Hanks, everybody!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:52.685 --> 00:01:02.695 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:05.264 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:07.900
-Hey, thank you!
Thank you!

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.702
Thank you!
-Welcome to the show!

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:12.204
-By the way --
All right, thank you.

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:15.074
Thanks!
The -- The perfect clip.

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:17.143
Was I in that clip?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:19.812
'Cause everybody's like,
"Wha? Wha?"

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:22.548
-Well, you do disappear
into this character, Tom.

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:23.616
-Oh, I don't disappear.

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:26.619
I am laid beneath
five hours of makeup.

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:28.688
-Was it five hours?
I was watching the film,

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:30.690
and there were moments
where it was really

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.525
just your voice and your eyes.

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:35.361
-Well, when I met -- excuse me.
I got to turn off my phone.

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:37.596
-Thank you so much. I'm sorry
we didn't tell you earlier.

00:01:37.596 --> 00:01:40.566
-No phones.
When I first met

00:01:40.566 --> 00:01:43.469
with Baz Luhrmann, I said,
"Can I ask you a question, Baz?"

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:45.070
"Yes, yes. What is it?
What is it?"

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:48.241
"Why are you talking to me about
a movie about Elvis Presley?

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:51.210
'Cause I don't see any --
who am I gonna play?

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.212
J. Edgar Hoover?"
-Right.

00:01:53.212 --> 00:01:56.048
-"Ike Eisenhower?
Where am I in this?"

00:01:56.048 --> 00:01:59.485
And he said, "No, this is about
Colonel Tom Parker and Elvis."

00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:01.387
I said, "Ah, all right.

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:03.256
Could you do me a favor, please?

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:05.358
Show me what Colonel Tom Parker
looks like?"

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:07.059
'Cause I'd never seen
a photograph of him,

00:02:07.059 --> 00:02:08.327
never heard his voice.

00:02:08.327 --> 00:02:09.795
You know, I think,
"Colonel Tom Parker --

00:02:09.795 --> 00:02:11.397
what is he? Does he make chicken
on the weekend?"

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.132
-Of course.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:16.168
-"Is he a big, big cowboy hat
and a stentorian voice?"

00:02:16.168 --> 00:02:17.637
And then
he showed me a photograph,

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:19.471
and so, again,
I had another question --

00:02:19.471 --> 00:02:22.875
"Why is it you're talking to me
about playing this guy?"

00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:24.777
-Were you offended
when he showed you the photo?

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:27.013
-I was not offended.
I said, "Ah, you see me

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:28.681
as a man
of infinite opportunity."

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:29.915
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.915 --> 00:02:32.252
All you have to do is say,
"Tom, Tom, just sit

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:34.921
in this chair for five hours,
and we'll do all the rest."

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:36.322
And that's
what we did every day.

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:37.457
And everybody says,
"How'd you get through

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:39.292
five hours of makeup?"
Like this.

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:41.361
This is how I got through
five hours of makeup.

00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:44.731
[ Laughter ]

00:02:44.731 --> 00:02:45.998
That's it.
That's all you have to do.

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:46.999
-That's the secret.

00:02:46.999 --> 00:02:49.068
-Cut to that clip
that you just saw.

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:50.603
-So, it's interesting
you say that,

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:53.038
'cause I obviously knew the name
Colonel Tom Parker,

00:02:53.038 --> 00:02:55.874
but I realized I had not seen
a photo of him,

00:02:55.874 --> 00:02:57.543
and I had never heard
what he'd sounded like.

00:02:57.543 --> 00:03:00.779
But do you think that's common
for people, that they never...?

00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:03.416
-Well, I think
the Elvis aficionados --

00:03:03.416 --> 00:03:04.784
We were just down in Memphis.

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:07.153
By the way, every person
in Memphis knows everything

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:09.155
there is to know
about Elvis Presley --

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:10.957
and FedEx, oddly enough.

00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:12.291
They just know everything.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:13.692
-So you're a huge deal
down there.

00:03:13.692 --> 00:03:17.730
-Oh, man, I can't -- I can't
buy a beer in Memphis, man.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:22.068
I cannot buy a chicken pot pie
in that town.

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:23.903
It's all brought over to me.

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:26.306
So, no, they do know,
but I'm just --

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:27.706
you know, I only knew Elvis

00:03:27.706 --> 00:03:31.411
from really silly,
kind of enjoyable movies.

00:03:31.411 --> 00:03:33.880
Elvis, to me, was the star
of "Girls! Girls! Girls!"

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.548
and "Fun in Acapulco."
-Uh-huh.

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:37.249
-"Spinout."

00:03:37.249 --> 00:03:40.220
The music -- you know,
I was a pre-Beatles -- that --

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.188
I turned 7
when the Beatles came out,

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:43.522
and they wiped out everything.

00:03:43.522 --> 00:03:45.858
Everything prior to the Beatles
was Doris Day.

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:48.427
-Yeah.
-Or, you know, Rosemary Clooney,

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:50.630
"How Much is that Doggie
in the Win--" including Elvis.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.198
You know,
that's just the way it is.

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:54.967
So I had no knowledge of it.

00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:57.703
And finding out
that Colonel Tom Parker's name

00:03:57.703 --> 00:03:59.238
was not Colonel Tom Parker --

00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:01.474
it was actually
Andreas Van Kuijk.

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:03.041
He was from Holland.
He jumped ship.

00:04:03.041 --> 00:04:06.679
He did not have a passport.
He was an illegal alien

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:10.082
and was named some sort of
bogus Kentucky colonel by the --

00:04:10.082 --> 00:04:13.419
I don't know, the Lexington
city council one day.

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.655
Turned that into a military rank

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:17.823
and called himself
a technical advisor.

00:04:17.823 --> 00:04:19.125
-When you got --
This is the first time

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:20.426
you worked with Baz Luhrmann,

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:22.394
and obviously,
you've seen his films.

00:04:22.394 --> 00:04:24.463
I can only imagine
what it's like to work --

00:04:24.463 --> 00:04:25.698
They're so unique.

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:27.133
Was working with him
what you expected?

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.335
-Baz makes coffee nervous.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:31.671
He is the hardest-working guy
there is.

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:34.172
And this is so funny.
So we --

00:04:34.172 --> 00:04:35.408
Austin, who played Elvis,

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:36.775
he worked on this movie
for two years.

00:04:36.775 --> 00:04:38.711
I was on it
for about six months.

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:40.846
And he would --
he would always be saying,

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:44.650
"I don't want to see --
What page is this now?

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.452
You just wait.
We'll wait, we'll wait.

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:47.920
You'll see new pages
in two weeks.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.254
Two weeks,
there'll be new pages."

00:04:49.254 --> 00:04:52.624
There was never two weeks.
No new pages ever came in.

00:04:52.624 --> 00:04:53.959
We'd come in
at the beginning of the day.

00:04:53.959 --> 00:04:55.761
He'd say, "Okay, today's work.
Have you seen the pages?"

00:04:55.761 --> 00:04:57.230
"Yeah, I have."
"Well, we're not doing them.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.597
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:00.666
"We're gonna do these --
we're gonna do these instead."

00:05:00.666 --> 00:05:05.404
It was like every day was
a performance of "La bohème."

00:05:05.404 --> 00:05:06.772
There's 8 million people.

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:09.075
There's no time and space.
There's no lot --

00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:10.309
I mean, as an actor,
it's, "Okay.

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:11.544
What's the geography
of the scene?

00:05:11.544 --> 00:05:13.546
What's the logic of the time?
What's the --"

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:17.683
Threw that all out the window
because he jumps around,

00:05:17.683 --> 00:05:20.485
and it's like -- there's no
shuffling of the cards with him.

00:05:20.485 --> 00:05:21.787
It's just go, go, go, go.

00:05:21.787 --> 00:05:24.424
And we would get to it.
We'd be working all day,

00:05:24.424 --> 00:05:27.526
and we'd be --
you know, we'd be in the --

00:05:27.526 --> 00:05:30.930
You know, about take 17,
"Baz, do we have this yet?

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.098
Take 17?
Are we done yet?

00:05:33.098 --> 00:05:34.834
I know we've done it differently
every time."

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:36.668
He'd say, "No, no, it's perfect.
Perfect.

00:05:36.668 --> 00:05:37.903
Absolutely perfect.
Perfect.

00:05:37.903 --> 00:05:39.272
I'm going to use it.
It's in the movie.

00:05:39.272 --> 00:05:41.107
It's in the movie.

00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:43.008
Just one enhancement,"
and then we'd do it

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:44.911
16 more times...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.911 --> 00:05:47.079
with more enhancement.
But the liberating quality

00:05:47.079 --> 00:05:50.449
of that meant that
we couldn't do anything wrong.

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:52.418
Everything was just grand.
So you never got a sense of,

00:05:52.418 --> 00:05:53.853
"Oh, that --
Can we start again?"

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:55.421
You never had to --
Because guess what --

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:57.156
you were starting again.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.156 --> 00:05:59.358
There was no need to ask
if you were going to.

00:05:59.358 --> 00:06:02.194
-There were -- the Vegas scenes

00:06:02.194 --> 00:06:05.298
were such a wonderful
cinematic experience.

00:06:05.298 --> 00:06:08.067
Seeing it on a big screen,
which I was lucky enough to do,

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:10.135
it was --
you felt like you were there.

00:06:10.135 --> 00:06:12.271
You felt like you were seeing
something special in real time.

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:14.539
And again, Austin,
I can't say enough about

00:06:14.539 --> 00:06:15.607
the transformation he did,
as well.

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:16.976
I know less --
I don't want to give him

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:18.944
too much credit because it
wasn't five hours of makeup.

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:20.479
-No, he did have a lot of time.

00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:22.114
He was in makeup
as long as I was.

00:06:22.114 --> 00:06:25.017
We were in COVID protocols,
so we didn't get --

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:27.320
Half the making
of the movie is --

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.355
Okay, one half
is free sandwiches

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:31.424
and haircuts anytime you want.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:31.424 --> 00:06:33.159
That's a big thing
about making movies.

00:06:33.159 --> 00:06:35.527
Don't tell anybody.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.527 --> 00:06:36.629
Otherwise everybody's gonna
want to be in the movies.

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:37.930
-Oh, yeah, everybody's
gonna want --

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.098
Once they hear about
the free-sandwich part, yeah.

00:06:39.098 --> 00:06:40.566
-That's all they want.
That's it.

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:42.868
You know, you know, a ride to
the airport when you need one.

00:06:42.868 --> 00:06:46.172
That's a good thing, too.
But the other thing is the hang.

00:06:46.172 --> 00:06:48.340
The hang of everybody
making a movie, man.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:51.444
It's just like, I mean,
the very first time Edison

00:06:51.444 --> 00:06:54.079
put people together
to make a movie and [trills]

00:06:54.079 --> 00:06:56.348
they said, "Hey, this is
a pretty good hang."

00:06:56.348 --> 00:06:57.883
But with the COVID protocols,

00:06:57.883 --> 00:06:59.451
we were not allowed
to see each other.

00:06:59.451 --> 00:07:01.354
-That's a real --
-So I was with --

00:07:01.354 --> 00:07:04.056
I was like this,
and there was Shaun,

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:05.958
and there was Jason,
and there was Brittany behind,

00:07:05.958 --> 00:07:07.159
holding my head.

00:07:07.159 --> 00:07:10.863
So, particularly
in the Las Vegas Hilton scenes,

00:07:10.863 --> 00:07:13.098
there's easily 300 extras there.

00:07:13.098 --> 00:07:14.967
They're all dress extras.
We're all ready,

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:18.371
and we are all waiting
for Elvis to show up.

00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:21.607
And he's on the other side of
the gold curtain, the whole bit.

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:23.976
And when you're waiting
for 45 minutes and the --

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:26.312
Can you guys just do that --
that audience-waiting

00:07:26.312 --> 00:07:27.713
"huzzah buzzah?"

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:30.082
Pretend the show hasn't started.
Just talk amongst yourself.

00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:32.417
[ Audience murmuring ]
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:32.417 --> 00:07:34.853
-Right.
There was that sound, you know?

00:07:34.853 --> 00:07:36.989
By the way, good job, guys.
That's fabulous.

00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:38.123
[ Applause ]
-They really --

00:07:38.123 --> 00:07:40.759
-That's good, that's good.

00:07:40.759 --> 00:07:44.796
So, we're waiting for him
to come out all morning long.

00:07:44.796 --> 00:07:48.200
And lo and behold, if we didn't
get the whole shebang --

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.336
♪ B-u-u-uh buh ♪

00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:51.570
Can you give me some timpani?

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:53.672
Kind of like
"2001: A Space Odyssey"?

00:07:53.672 --> 00:07:55.540
You know...
♪ Bum -- ♪

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:57.609
[ Drumming ]
Yeah, that.

00:07:57.609 --> 00:08:00.212
And so we're all getting paid
to be there, right?

00:08:00.212 --> 00:08:01.981
And we're all gonna be there
all day long.

00:08:01.981 --> 00:08:04.650
But every time that began --
one more time.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.084
[ Drumming ]
Yeah, great.

00:08:06.084 --> 00:08:07.587
And the curtain would suddenly
come up,

00:08:07.587 --> 00:08:09.121
we'd start hearing the band,
my God,

00:08:09.121 --> 00:08:11.057
it was like we were transported.

00:08:11.057 --> 00:08:12.758
300 people were going gaga.

00:08:12.758 --> 00:08:15.928
And we got to watch the show
again and again and again

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:17.129
and again and again.

00:08:17.129 --> 00:08:18.864
And every time,
it was just magic.

00:08:18.864 --> 00:08:20.733
-I can imagine.
I will say the whole time

00:08:20.733 --> 00:08:23.035
I was watching it --
I'm genuinely saying this --

00:08:23.035 --> 00:08:24.670
I thought, "I cannot wait
to see this again."

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:28.507
-We forget the power of Elvis
in a room in Las Vegas.

00:08:28.507 --> 00:08:30.643
It's kind of, like,
developed a kind of like a --

00:08:30.643 --> 00:08:32.644
more like a --
-Yeah, kitschy, yeah.

00:08:32.644 --> 00:08:35.781
-It was not in the day.
Anybody around the world,

00:08:35.781 --> 00:08:37.015
if they could jump through

00:08:37.015 --> 00:08:38.950
a time-and-space-continuum
machine,

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:40.386
"Where would you
like to be today?"

00:08:40.386 --> 00:08:44.122
"I would like to be in Las Vegas
seeing my idol, Elvis Presley.

00:08:44.122 --> 00:08:45.424
That is where
I would like to be."

00:08:45.424 --> 00:08:46.425
And they all went.

00:08:46.425 --> 00:08:47.926
-Well, I have a lot more
to ask you.

00:08:47.926 --> 00:08:49.095
-Bring it on!
-We're gonna stick around.

00:08:49.095 --> 00:08:50.162
We'll be right back
with more from Tom Hanks.

00:08:50.162 --> 00:08:51.797
-Huzzah. Huzzah.
-Huzzah, clap.

00:08:51.797 --> 00:08:53.232
Clap, clap, clap.

